WEBVTT

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:20.900
Cheyenne I'll get you out kind of had you in a tough spot come on

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.560
You can't bring him back, old boy.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.510
You'd better come with me.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:46.550
Come on.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.120
Come on.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:54.950
There, sir boy.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.960
We are going to be pals.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.820
Howdy, Bill.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:18.160
Hello, Tex.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.190
Are you the only survivor of this outfit?

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:22.190
No, Bill.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.940
I wasn't traveling with a train.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.130
This little fella here is the only survivor.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.330
Cheyennes, eh?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.920
Yep. It looks like Howling Wolf is on the warpath again.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:34.320
Sure does.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.680
And the old devil signed the peace treaty barely a month ago.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:41.030
Just another problem for Buffalo Bill to uncoil.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.750
Better come and help me, Tex.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.750
We'll drop by Fort Stanton.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.280
You know, Captain Taylor thinks Tex Houston's the greatest Indian scout this side of the Pecos.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:50.680
I wish I could.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.590
But I'm due in Plainsville before noon, May 1st.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:54.910
You better start making tracks.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.750
Don't let the Cheyennes lift that long, nice hair of yours.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.000
Ha, ha, ha, ha.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:00.870
So long, Bill.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.060
Good luck, Tex.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.040
time for the help. I'm done for.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:29.670
Oh, come on, partner. Brace up. It takes more than an Indian area to down a Pony Express rider.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:37.170
I know. The mail. It's got to get through. Take it. Will ya?

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:19.400
well partner you sure acted right sudden that's a good name for you sudden

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:51.520
You ready to go, Johnson?

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.820
- It will be, Mr. Hawkins.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:55.660
I got a seat and about more grub and supplies I need.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.450
- Better tend to it right away, then.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.920
We ought to pull out of here in the morning.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.000
- Don't worry.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:21.830
I need to go with a wagon train tomorrow and he needs the more supplies and his money's

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:22.830
by now.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:23.830
No credit.

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:24.890
But Mr. Eaton as soon as I get it.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:25.890
I told you no credit.

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:27.010
Good day sir.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:34.400
You know the rules, Grant. Why do you waste my time?

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.660
But the poor fellow's short of food supplies, and I thought...

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.920
I'll do the thinking around here. You worry about your own troubles.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:45.200
You know what day this is? The 1st of May. Almost noon.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:50.020
The deadline for you to pay back that $3,000 you stole from me a year ago.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.360
Mr. Eaton, you know I didn't steal that money.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.850
Sure. Your son Tom might have done it.

00:07:55.450 --> 00:08:00.000
In fact, he wrote a confession saying he stole the money, and then left town hoping

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.740
to save you by pinning the crime on himself. The shortage is still on your books. And unless

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.700
you make good by 12 o'clock, you go to prison. But Mr. Eaton, how can I make it good? There

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:16.680
just isn't any way. That's where you're wrong, Grant. There is a way. I told you before I'm

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:23.470
not going to force my daughter to marry against her will. Well, speaking of angels, your father

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:30.000
and I were just talking about you, Mary. He suggests that you and I, won't you come into

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:36.610
talk you might have a little talk with daddy first why certainly not thanks

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:40.820
child but there's nothing you can do there is something I can do and I'm

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:46.320
going to do it no Mary but dad he'll put you in prison I wouldn't mind that so

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.430
much if Tom was here to look after you can't understand why we haven't heard

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:58.260
from him in all these months especially when he knows the circumstances

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:30.000
in judge and you too Bart say you ain't the regular I

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:35.790
up in the jack union's going to get jack was a good boy but it's all the day's work

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:40.030
I reckon all new and distribute the mail with you while I get this fellow's report

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:46.440
on what happened come in my office I wish there was a letter for you but it just ain't

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:57.930
I can't understand why Tom don't run what time is it ten minutes to twelve I'll have

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.000
to hurry does Andy Grant still live here

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.400
here yeah I don't eat you know no I've got a very important letter for him from

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:09.870
his son well and it'd be right glad to hear from Tom he's been expecting a

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:17.550
letter I give it to me and I'll take it over to him I'll take it all thanks just

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:23.080
the same

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:38.720
Conexpress writer has a letter from Tom Grant with some valuable information in his saddlebags

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:43.630
Keep him here till I get it keep him here. Yes agitate a bull rush work the crowd up

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:49.280
Be sure to keep him inside right. Hey, it's just down the street

00:10:55.190 --> 00:11:00.000
I don't know anything about gold in California or anything

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:03.740
- He's just trying to hold out on us.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.360
- That's right, let's make him talk.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.970
Come on, out with it.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:11.450
- All right, I can't fool you fellas, can I?

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:13.900
Well, I'll tell you all about it.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:17.290
- No, out in Gold Creek, California,

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:20.970
the gold is so thick, they only pick up the big pieces.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:25.410
Let the little ones lay.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:38.430
No, Mary. I'll not let you go through with it.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:41.260
Please, Father. May I see you a moment, Mr. Eaton?

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:45.460
I've changed my mind. I'm willing to marry you.

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:46.700
Where will I find Mr. Grant?

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:48.320
I'm Grant. What can I do for you?

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:52.260
I'm Tex Houston. I've got a letter for you from your son.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.440
From Tom? You've seen him? Where is he?

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.580
In Gold Creek, California.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.120
Oh, Dad, I knew he wouldn't fail us. Is he all right?

00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.830
I'll see. And he struck it rich.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.530
So you're Tom's sister.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:05.020
I've heard a lot about you.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:09.250
Well, I've got that letter here somewhere.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:17.010
Anyway, here's a sack full of gold nuggets he sent you.

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:20.330
You see, Mr. Eaton, we Grants keep our word.

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:22.380
So you're Bart Eaton?

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:24.040
That's my name, stranger.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:29.590
Why, this isn't gold.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:37.480
It's nothing but a handful of worthless rocks that don't even show color.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:48.170
The letter's gone too. Somebody's rifled my saddlebag.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:49.770
That's a likely story.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:57.770
Mr. Grant, before I left Gold Creek, your son put $5,000 worth of gold nuggets in that sack.

00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:00.000
Enough for you to pay off eating and bring your new...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.040
your daughter out west. You expect us

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:03.940
to believe that? There never was

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.920
any gold. Then why did Mr. Houston come here

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:07.540
at all, saying he's a friend of Tom's?

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:09.690
To stall for time. The whole story

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:11.410
is a put-up job between this fella

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:12.850
and that crooked brother of yours.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:14.410
Why, you...

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:30.000
All that matter.

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:34.020
in jail, Ed. I charge you with assault and battery. Come along with me, stranger. Just a minute,

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:41.050
Marshal. He was only standing up for my brother, Tom. Never mind, Miss Grant.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:57.510
I'll be back shortly, Grant, for a showdown with you. Oldie Express

00:13:57.510 --> 00:13:59.600
Riders are a pretty tough customer, Aiden.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.690
I've got him where he won't bother us anymore.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:08.900
Well, Grant, your son's bluff didn't work.

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:10.850
Have you made up your mind what you're going to do about it?

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:15.680
Get it over, send me to prison.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:19.140
Haven't you anything to suggest, Mary?

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:24.290
Hey, the wagon train's pulling out!

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:26.530
Thought they weren't leaving 'til tomorrow.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.520
They're hitting the trail for Gold Creek.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:30.000
That's strangest talk of the Gold Creek.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:32.180
has sure stampeded him.

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:34.780
How about it, Mary?

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.340
Are you ready to marry me?

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:38.850
I'll give you my answers this time tomorrow.

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:39.730
Very well.

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:40.870
This time tomorrow.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.900
I found a note from Mary saying she was going to spend the night with friends.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:04.900
She should be here right now to give me her answer.

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:06.290
Oh, Mr. Eaton?

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:06.890
Yes?

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:08.930
Mary Grant asked me to give you this.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:09.830
Where is she?

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.570
I don't know where she is now. She gave me that yesterday.

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:14.040
Thank you.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:23.350
Spent the night with friends, eh?

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.180
Ran away with a wagon train, you mean?

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.280
She's not running away. She's going to find her brother.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.660
That won't save you this time.

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:33.320
You're going to prison.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:43.060
I knew you'd find me, old boy.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.640
I bet you looked all over town for me.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:48.190
Well, I've been kind of worried about you.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.160
Someone's coming.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:52.840
Now's your chance to sneak in.

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:04.980
Hey, jailer, where are you?

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:13.530
I'll put you in here in the other prison, Grant.

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:18.520
Well, Mr. Grant, what are they putting you in here for?

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:20.970
Marty who carried out his dress.

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:24.100
I'll teach that jailer to stay on the job.

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:29.440
Well...

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:33.480
Anyway, your daughter's out of Bart Eaton's reach by now.

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:36.470
That's just it. She's not. Eaton's going after her.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:40.660
What? We've got to get out of here.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:46.260
Get out? I'll kill her without help.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:48.660
I've got it.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:55.550
Sutton. Come here, boy. Sutton, old boy. Come here. Come here, boy. Come here.

00:16:55.550 --> 00:17:00.000
Where have you been? Where have you been, huh? Glad to see you.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:00.920
See those keys up there?

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:03.440
I want you to jump up there and get them.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:05.120
Jump high and bring them back to me.

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:05.870
Sorry.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:08.960
That's the boy.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:09.940
Now bring them here.

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:12.930
Thank you, pal.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:14.570
That's a nice boy.

00:17:14.950 --> 00:17:23.080
What is it, Tex?

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:24.900
Indian smoke signals.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:25.820
Can you read them?

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:28.980
They are going to attack the wagon train.

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:53.330
- Come on, we've got to warn them.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:55.110
- Well, Mary, enjoy your trip.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:56.200
- For Mr. Eaton.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:57.500
- Surprised to see me, eh?

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:59.860
Oh, you'll see a lot of me before we get to California.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.740
You mean, you're riding with the wagon train?

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:02.360
Yes.

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.590
A lot of these freight wagons belong to me.

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:08.070
Besides, that isn't the only interest I have in this train.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:10.440
See you later.

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:12.530
Get up!

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:26.870
Two riders are coming.

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:29.410
Down now, but they're sure burning the wind.

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:50.840
They're coming to attack the wagons.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:52.600
Houston here read their signal.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:53.940
How the wagons form a circle?

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:55.310
We haven't got time for that.

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:57.330
Our only chance is to head for the river.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:58.640
If we haven't got time to get across,

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:00.000
at least they can't surround us.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:02.530
Take care of an Indian fighter, Hawkins!

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:04.050
All right, Houston, take command!

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:05.050
Head to the river!

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:07.130
Right to the river!

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:08.230
Get up!

00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:11.270
Get up!

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:32.600
- I got that in.
